WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
A pond drop may hold cells from across life.

2
00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:05.620
Which question should we ask first?

3
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:10.100
The cells drift together, but a useful key needs an order.

4
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:15.320
We will follow one mystery cell and let each answer open the next path.

5
00:00:18.817 --> 00:00:22.557
Start with what a light microscope can show about cell organization.

6
00:00:24.077 --> 00:00:27.557
A nucleus is a membrane-wrapped compartment holding the DNA.

7
00:00:29.037 --> 00:00:31.557
If the cell has one, it belongs in Eukarya.

8
00:00:32.857 --> 00:00:35.757
If it does not, the organism is a prokaryote.

9
00:00:36.957 --> 00:00:38.997
Its DNA sits in the cytoplasm.

10
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.190
Our mystery cell has no nucleus.

11
00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:46.710
That rules out Eukarya, but Bacteria and Archaea remain.

12
00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.170
Both domains are made up of prokaryotes.

13
00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.970
Most are single cells, though some grow in chains or filaments.

14
00:00:56.290 --> 00:01:00.270
Under a classroom microscope, members of these domains can look alike.

15
00:01:01.383 --> 00:01:03.243
Look at these nucleus-free cells.

16
00:01:04.623 --> 00:01:06.563
Their shapes and sizes appear similar.

17
00:01:07.883 --> 00:01:12.103
Can the microscope alone place one in Bacteria and the other in Archaea?

18
00:01:13.263 --> 00:01:15.963
Choose yes or no, and name the evidence you trust.

19
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.600
No. Appearance has reached its limit, so the key must change evidence.

20
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.380
Ribosomal RNA offers one test.

21
00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.580
Every cell uses this molecule to build proteins.

22
00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:40.360
Its sequence patterns differ between Bacteria and Archaea.

23
00:01:41.283 --> 00:01:45.583
Picture each sequence as a labelled strip, not a molecular structure.

24
00:01:47.063 --> 00:01:49.523
Matching positions line up while differences flash.

25
00:01:51.303 --> 00:01:54.043
Scientists compare these patterns to judge relatedness.

26
00:01:55.163 --> 00:02:00.263
Early comparisons separated methane-making microbes from typical bacteria.

27
00:02:01.563 --> 00:02:03.883
Where looks failed, the molecules still differed.

28
00:02:04.967 --> 00:02:07.187
Cell chemistry adds another route.

29
00:02:08.487 --> 00:02:11.427
Most bacteria have peptidoglycan in their walls.

30
00:02:13.027 --> 00:02:14.027
Archaeal walls have none.

31
00:02:15.247 --> 00:02:18.627
In a membrane lipid, a chemical link holds the fatty tails.

32
00:02:20.327 --> 00:02:24.287
Typical bacteria use ester links; typical archaea use ether links.

33
00:02:25.567 --> 00:02:27.427
No single sketch proves identity.

34
00:02:28.787 --> 00:02:30.507
Use the full pattern of evidence.

35
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:37.420
Now the mystery cell carries an archaeal sequence pattern, no peptidoglycan, and

36
00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.480
typical ether-linked membrane lipids.

37
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.580
Together those traits support Archaea.

38
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.660
A different pattern would support Bacteria.

39
00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:50.220
The gate opens only when the evidence supports the placement.

40
00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.240
Place the domains on branches, not steps.

41
00:02:55.820 --> 00:03:00.240
Sequence comparisons show relatedness, not a ladder from simple to advanced.

42
00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:02.980
All branches share a root.

43
00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:05.700
The course drawing is simplified.

44
00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.860
Archaea and Eukarya are more closely related to each other than either is to

45
00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:12.860
Bacteria.

46
00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:15.000
This path has a limit.

47
00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:21.140
It places an unknown in the course's domain scheme; it does not identify every

48
00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:25.000
species. Keep the method, not a picture of rank.

49
00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:30.580
Classify by ordered traits; where looks fail, use molecular evidence.
